      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Method"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Mix the dark soy sauce, light soy sauce, palm sugar, and crushed garlic in a bowl. Add the pork belly and turn to coat. Leave to marinate for at least 30 minutes or overnight."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Heat the oven to 200 C. Place the pork on a baking tray. Roast for 40 to 45 minutes, turning and basting with the marinade every 15 minutes, until the surface is dark, lacquered, and fragrant."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Rest the pork on a board for 10 minutes. Collect all the resting juices."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "For the gravy: pour the resting juices into a small saucepan. Add 200 ml of water, the extra dark soy, light soy, and palm sugar. Bring to a simmer. Stir in the dissolved cornflour and cook for 2 minutes until the gravy thickens slightly."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Slice the pork thinly across the grain. Serve over bowls of {{jasmine-rice}}. Spoon the warm gravy over the pork. Top with spring onions and coriander. Add halved boiled eggs if desired."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "troubleshooter",
        "attrs": {
          "items": [
            {
              "symptom": "Pork surface is not lacquered and dark",
              "cause": "Not enough basting or oven temperature too low",
              "fix": "Baste every 15 minutes. If the surface is pale at 30 minutes, increase the oven to 220 C for the final 10 minutes."
            },
            {
              "symptom": "Gravy is too thin",
              "cause": "Cornflour not fully dissolved before adding",
              "fix": "Mix cornflour thoroughly with cold water before adding to the warm gravy. Simmer for at least 2 minutes."
            },
            {
              "symptom": "Pork is dry when sliced",
              "cause": "Resting time skipped or pork overcooked",
              "fix": "Always rest for at least 10 minutes. The internal temperature at the thickest point should reach 75 C."
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Where this dish lives"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Khao moo dang is a staple of Bangkok's Chinatown-influenced rice and pork shops. The dish shows how Chinese barbecue techniques were absorbed into Thai street food culture. The red colour of the pork comes from the soy and sugar marinade caramelising in the oven. In Thailand it is often sold alongside white braised pork (khao moo krob) and crispy pork at the same stall. The sweet soy gravy poured over the rice is as important as the pork itself."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}
